    description: "[REVIEW 2026-11-01, CURRENT] Reviewed 2026-11-01 on dev 100b6fbb6e. rust.yaml:370-380 still has no std::process grant, and test_a_pid_is_not_rowed_by_python_precedent still exists. Fixture env::var then process::exit: the \"never launches a subprocess\" claim is inconclusive (\"could not classify (std::env, std::process)\"). An elixir System.halt fixture is also inconclusive. Correction: python.yaml:1862-1870 grants os completeness: complete with getpid deliberately unrowed. Python's precedent is therefore grant-with-pid-inert, which undercuts the reason rust gives for withholding the grant. (Original text below; the review entry in the thread has the evidence.)\n\nFiled by the mulul run as a REFUSAL, recorded as a decision (LIVE rule: a refusal files as one).\n\nINV-mulul removed rust std::process [exit, abort] from subprocess. The haskell (System.Exit, INV-dozuj) and javascript (process) precedents ALSO add a module_completeness grant so the now-unrowed call is an EXAMINED negative. For rust that was not done: std::process (exact-match module) also exports id() -- the pid -- which rust.yaml holds UNROWED and test_rust_process_env_surface.py::test_a_pid_is_not_rowed_by_python_precedent pins unrowed (\"python deliberately holds os.getpid out\"), while c rows unistd.getpid and javascript rows process.pid as host_info_read, and python.yaml's own os note says the reason for holding getpid out \"IS GONE (INV-tutar)\" and that adding it would be an ordinary row. A completeness grant with id unrowed would declare the pid not-I/O.\n\nCONSEQUENCE, measured on a one-file fixture (env::var -> process::exit): \"never launches a subprocess\" and host_secret -> subprocess are now INCONCLUSIVE (\"calls into module(s) the I/O catalog could not classify (std::env, std::process)\"), not confirmed. Same for elixir System.halt (module System).\n\nDECISION NEEDED: row a pid read (rust std::process::id, python os.getpid / getppid) as host_info_read across catalogues (the c / javascript reading), then grant std::process complete with exit/abort declared not-I/O. Not done on the mulul branch because it is an ADDITION across languages and reverses a pinned test."  # e9ce
